---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import VoltageDropTool from '../../components/tools/VoltageDropTool.astro';

const canonical = 'https://electrasim.com/tools/voltage-drop-calculator/';
const title = 'Voltage Drop Calculator — Free Electrical Tool | ElectraSim';
const description =
  'Calculate voltage drop in a cable run and see exactly how cable length, current and cable size affect voltage at the load. Free interactive electrical calculator.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'Voltage Drop Calculator',
      url: canonical,
      applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Web',
      description,
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'ElectraSim', url: 'https://electrasim.com/' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'What is voltage drop?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Voltage drop is the voltage lost in the cable between the supply and the load, caused by the resistance of the conductors. The load always sees less than the supply voltage.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'What increases voltage drop?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'A longer cable run, a higher load current, or a smaller conductor cross-section all increase voltage drop. A larger conductor reduces it.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'How is voltage drop calculated?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Resistance R = rho x L / A, where L is the round-trip conductor length (one-way length doubled). Voltage drop is then I x R, and the percentage is the drop divided by the supply voltage.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://electrasim.com/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Electrical Toolbox', item: 'https://electrasim.com/tools/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Voltage Drop Calculator', item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<ToolLayout toolId="voltage-drop" title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <VoltageDropTool />
</ToolLayout>

<article class="tool-article">
  <div class="tool-article-in">
    <h2>How this voltage drop calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Current flowing through a cable meets resistance, and that resistance turns part of your
      supply voltage into heat. What is left is the voltage the load actually receives. This tool
      models that relationship and shows it changing as you edit the inputs.
    </p>
    <p>The calculation is deliberately simple and fully documented:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Round-trip conductor length: <code>L = one-way length × 2</code> — current flows out and back.</li>
      <li>Cable resistance: <code>R = ρ × L ÷ A</code></li>
      <li>Voltage drop: <code>V<sub>drop</sub> = I × R</code></li>
      <li>Percentage: <code>V<sub>drop</sub> ÷ V<sub>source</sub> × 100</code></li>
      <li>Voltage at the load: <code>V<sub>source</sub> − V<sub>drop</sub></code></li>
      <li>Resistive loss: <code>P = I² × R</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Resistivity is fixed at <code>0.017241 Ω·mm²/m</code> for copper and
      <code>0.028264 Ω·mm²/m</code> for aluminium, both at 20 °C. Every figure on screen comes from
      the same calculated resistance.
    </p>

    <h2>What is voltage drop?</h2>
    <p>
      Voltage drop is the difference between the voltage leaving the supply and the voltage arriving
      at the equipment. A 230 V supply with a 3% drop delivers about 223 V. Too much drop makes
      motors run hot, lamps dim, and heaters underperform.
    </p>

    <h2>What increases voltage drop?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Longer cable runs</strong> — resistance is proportional to length.</li>
      <li><strong>Higher current</strong> — drop is proportional to current.</li>
      <li><strong>Smaller conductors</strong> — resistance is inversely proportional to area.</li>
      <li><strong>Aluminium instead of copper</strong> — roughly 64% more resistive for the same size.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 230 V single-phase supply feeding 40 A through 50 m of 10 mm² copper:
      the conductor path is 100 m, so <code>R = 0.017241 × 100 ÷ 10 = 0.17241 Ω</code>.
      The drop is <code>40 × 0.17241 = 6.90 V</code>, which is <strong>3.00%</strong> of 230 V,
      leaving <strong>223.10 V</strong> at the load and dissipating <strong>275.86 W</strong> in the cable.
    </p>

    <h2>About the 3% reference figure</h2>
    <p>
      The tool compares your result against a 3% educational reference threshold. That is a teaching
      aid, not a compliance verdict. Real limits depend on the applicable wiring rules, the circuit
      type and the installation. This v1 model is purely resistive — it does not account for cable
      reactance, power factor, temperature correction, grouping or harmonics.
    </p>

    <h2>Keep learning</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/blog/voltage-drop-explained-how-to-calculate-it/">Voltage drop explained — how to calculate it</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/electrical-cable-sizes-explained/">Electrical cable sizes explained</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/ohms-law-explained-voltage-current-resistance/">Ohm's Law explained</a></li>
      <li><a href="/tools/">Back to the Electrical Toolbox</a></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tool-article-note">
      <h3 class="mt0">Want to see the circuit?</h3>
      <p class="mb0">
        Take these values into ElectraSim and build the circuit interactively — place the supply,
        the protective device and the load, then energise it.
        <a href="/app/"><strong>Build it in ElectraSim →</strong></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
